# Reading a map into a name before putting into it makes native code
# roughly cubic. Reading inline in the put's argument stays linear.
#
# Run from this directory: kanso build main.kso --release && ./main, then
#  change 8000 in main.kso.
#
# should: both loops linear, as the inline one is.
# does (release, 2026-10-24, shared 4-CPU box): `named` 2,000 / 4,000 /
#   8,000 keys take 0.16 s / 1.22 s / 9.6 s; dev at 8,000 takes 4.5 s; the
#   interpreter takes 1.0 s. `inline` at 8,000 takes 0.012 s on release. With
#   40,000 iterations the release binary ran 219 s and was killed.
#
# The two functions differ only in whether `m[key]` gets a name. Naming it is
# what a reader does to keep a line short, and the 2026-10-12 check change
# that carries errs through names makes binding the ordinary style.
fn named m i n
  return m if n < i
  key = "k{i}"
  seen = m[key]
  named (put m key (counted seen)) (i + 1) n

fn inline m i n
  return m if n < i
  key = "k{i}"
  inline (put m key (counted m[key])) (i + 1) n

fn counted none
  1

fn counted c
  c + 1

pub fn named_n n
  length (named {} 1 n)

pub fn inline_n n
  length (inline {} 1 n)
